                <pb facs="0595" n="497" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg497"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/11889: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Amoy (Franklin) to the
                            Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Amoy</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-04-20T16:00:00+08:00">April 20, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 20—9:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of April 19, 3 p.m.,<anchor n="41" xml:id="d521-f1"/> it
                    is apparently true that Changchow has been captured. Refugees continue to flow
                    into Amoy and International Settlement of Kulangsu. Four British submarines with
                    mother ship arrived this afternoon en route to Weihai-wei for a visit of 3 days.
                    Two Japanese destroyers to have left this morning have received orders to remain
                    here. Americans at Tungan evacuating that place and coming to Kulangsu. My
                    despatch No. 12, dated December 31, 1931, file No. 130.7<anchor n="41"
                        xml:id="d521-f2"/> gives list of American citizens in this district with
                    their addresses in the United States.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Franklin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
